=base:summary.cmark
The zone-file format microdns reads its fixed records from: the supported record types, the apex syntax, and a commented example.

=base:main-content.cmark

# MicroZone files

microdns uses its own zone-file format, with limited support for DNS records:
all TTLs are 300, and there is no support for the CHAOS class, for instance.
The file is given with `--zoneFile` and read at startup; see
[Getting started](/getting-started.html).

An example file, with comments, is as follows. It is the repository's
[`fixtures/example.mzone`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/microdns/blob/main/fixtures/example.mzone).

```
-- comments start with "--"
-- all records have fixed TTL to 300
-- there are no wildcards
-- the @ special syntax replaces the current apex in some records (from the commandline or overriden)
-- otherwise, all domain names are fqdns

-- A and AAAA records
A example.com. 1.2.3.4
AAAA example.com. ::1.2.3.4

-- TXT wants quoted strings
TXT example.com. "microdns has \"quoted\" strings with \\ (backslashes)"
TXT @ "apex syntax"

-- CAA has two quoted strings
CAA example.com. "issue" "letsencrypt"

-- MX

MX example.com. 10 1.2.3.4
MX example.com. 100 spool.example.com.

-- CNAME

CNAME dir.example.com. directory.example.com

-- SRV have only priority
SRV _altweb._tcp.example.com. 10 8080 1.2.3.4

microdns:apex other.example.com.

TXT @ "apex syntax in other.example.com."
```

The `microdns:apex` directive changes the apex that `@` stands for in the
records that follow it; before any such directive, `@` is the apex given on
the command line with `--dnsApex`.

